@@s001 Currying and composition
@@s002 dato:value that enters,ora:function at work,ok:result
@@s003 <code>add</code> is a machine that takes <b>one</b> argument at a time.
@@s004 <code>add(2)</code>: the 2 enters and what comes out is not a number, but a <b>new machine</b>, <code>y =&gt; 2 + y</code>, with the 2 already inside (partial application).
@@s005 The second argument, 3, enters the new machine…
@@s006 …and <b>5</b> comes out. <code>add(2)(3)</code> = 5, like <code>add2(2, 3)</code>, but halfway through I had in hand a reusable function.
@@s007 <b>Composition</b>: <code>compose(square, abs)(-3)</code>. First abs works (the function on the right): −3 → 3. Then square: 3 → 9. "f after g".
@@s008 dato:argument,ora:function,due:function with an argument already inside,ok:result
@@s009 ora:still to produce,dato:I have in hand,ok:done
@@s010 dato:starting function,ora:transformation,ok:function obtained
@@s011 Currying and composition
@@s012 A function with two arguments can be rewritten as a function that takes <b>one</b> and returns another function that waits for the second. It is called <b>currying</b>. It serves to fix an argument once and reuse the rest.
@@s013 Two arguments together, or one at a time
@@s014 Together
@@s015 Type: <code>(Int, Int) => Int</code>. You must give both arguments at once.
@@s016 One at a time (curried)
@@s017 Type: <code>Int => (Int => Int)</code>. Each argument has its own pair of brackets.
@@s018 add(2)(3), one argument at a time
@@s019 <code>add</code> takes <code>x</code> and returns <b>everything that comes after the first arrow</b>.
@@s020 I give only the first argument. I get a function with the 2 already inside. It is called <b>partial application</b>.
@@s021 The second <code>(3)</code> calls that function.
@@s022 Same result as <code>add2(2, 3)</code>.
@@s023 The advantage: I fix the first argument once and get a new function, reusable.
@@s024 It works also with <code>def</code>s that have several parameter lists: <code>def add(x: Int)(y: Int): Int = x + y</code>, and then <code>val incr = add(1)</code> (checked with Scala 3.8).
@@s025 add2(2, 3) against add(2)(3)
@@s026 Writing <code>curry</code>: let the types decide
@@s027 Exercise 5. It looks hard, but the types tell you every piece. Look at what you must <b>produce</b> and build it from the outside in.
@@s028 curry built guided by the type
@@s029 dato:A,due:B,ok:C
@@s030 I write below what I must produce and what I have.
@@s031 The result is a function that takes an <code>A</code>. A function is written with the arrow: I start with <code>a =></code>.
@@s032 After receiving <code>a</code> I must give <code>B => C</code>: another function. I write <code>b =></code>.
@@s033 Now I must give a <code>C</code>. I have in hand only <code>a</code>, <code>b</code> and <code>f</code>.
@@s034 The only way to obtain a <code>C</code> is to call <code>f</code> with an A and a B. There were no other choices: the code was written in the type.
@@s035 Same method for <code>uncurry</code> (exercise 6), which makes the reverse trip:
@@s036 uncurry
@@s037 I must produce a function that takes <b>two</b> arguments together.
@@s038 So I write a function with two parameters.
@@s039 I have <code>f</code> that wants one argument at a time. <code>f(a)</code> gives me a function <code>B => C</code>…
@@s040 …which I call right away with <code>b</code>. I get the <code>C</code>.
@@s041 <code>curry</code> and <code>uncurry</code> are the inverse of each other: <code>uncurry(curry(f))</code> behaves like <code>f</code>. It is exactly what the tests of exercise 6 check.
@@s042 From types to code, one piece at a time
@@s043 <code>isSortedCurried</code>: reuse instead of rewriting
@@s044 Exercises 5 and 6: no new code
@@s045 The required type: first the array, then the criterion, then the result. It is the one-argument-at-a-time version of <code>isSorted</code>.
@@s046 I rewrite nothing: I take <code>isSorted</code> (with two arguments) and pass it to <code>curry</code>. The square brackets <code>[A]</code> say for which type.
@@s047 And going back with <code>uncurry</code> I get back a function equivalent to the original.
@@s048 Why it is useful
@@s049 The first argument can configure the function for the whole project, the second arrives at runtime. And often curry/uncurry serve only to make the types match when you pass a function to another that wants it in the other form.
@@s050 curry(isSorted) instead of rewriting
@@s051 Composing two functions
@@s052 Exercise 7: joining two functions in a row. Without using the library's <code>compose</code> or <code>andThen</code>.
@@s053 compose(square, abs)(-3)
@@s054 <code>f</code> = <code>square</code>, <code>g</code> = <code>abs</code>. The order follows mathematics: <span class="m">f ∘ g</span> is read "f after g".
@@s055 I substitute <code>f</code> and <code>g</code> in the body.
@@s056 <code>a</code> = -3. First the innermost function is computed: <code>g</code>.
@@s057 The result of <code>g</code> enters <code>f</code>. That is why the output type of <code>g</code> (<code>B</code>) must be the input type of <code>f</code>.
@@s058 End.
@@s059 Functions as machines
